{
  "version": 1,
  "id": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000302",
  "title": "Lagrange Points",
  "created_at": "2026-03-26T00:00:00Z",
  "updated_at": "2026-03-26T00:00:00Z",
  "shared_cargo_toml": "[profile.release]\nopt-level = 1\nlto = false\ncodegen-units = 16",
  "shared_source": null,
  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "cell_0",
      "order": 0,
      "label": "Introduction",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "# Lagrange Points\n\nIn celestial mechanics, the **Lagrange points** are five positions in a two-body gravitational system where a small object can remain stationary relative to the two large bodies.\n\n## The Circular Restricted Three-Body Problem (CR3BP)\n\nWe work in a **co-rotating frame** that spins with the two primary bodies ($M_1$, $M_2$). In this frame, both primaries are stationary and the dynamics of a test particle are governed by an effective potential that combines gravity and centrifugal effects:\n\n$$\\Phi(x, y) = -\\frac{1-\\mu}{r_1} - \\frac{\\mu}{r_2} - \\frac{1}{2}(x^2 + y^2)$$\n\nwhere $\\mu = M_2 / (M_1 + M_2)$ is the mass ratio, and $r_1$, $r_2$ are distances to each primary.\n\n## The Five Points\n\n| Point | Location | Stability |\n|-------|----------|-----------|\n| **L1** | Between the two bodies | Unstable (saddle) |\n| **L2** | Beyond the smaller body | Unstable (saddle) |\n| **L3** | Beyond the larger body | Unstable (saddle) |\n| **L4** | 60° ahead in orbit | Stable (for $\\mu < 0.0385$) |\n| **L5** | 60° behind in orbit | Stable (for $\\mu < 0.0385$) |\n\nL4 and L5 form equilateral triangles with the two primaries — these are the famous **Trojan points** where Jupiter's Trojan asteroids collect.\n\nRun the simulation below to watch test particles orbit near each Lagrange point in real time!",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_controls",
      "order": 1,
      "label": "Controls",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "ui::slider(\"speed\", 1.0, 20.0)\n    .step(1.0)\n    .label(\"Simulation Speed\")\n    .default_value(5.0)",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_sim",
      "order": 2,
      "label": "Live Simulation",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "// Lagrange Points — live simulation in the co-rotating frame.\n//\n// Shows two primaries (M1, M2), all five Lagrange points, and five\n// test particles (one near each L-point) evolving under the CR3BP\n// equations of motion with Coriolis and centrifugal forces.\n\nconst WIDTH: u32 = 800;\nconst HEIGHT: u32 = 800;\nconst MU: f64 = 0.012; // Earth-Moon-like mass ratio\nconst DT: f64 = 0.0005;\nconst MAX_TRAIL: usize = 600;\n\n// Map physics coordinates to pixel space.\n// The system spans roughly [-2, 2] x [-2, 2].\nconst SCALE: f64 = 180.0; // pixels per unit\nconst CX: f64 = 400.0;   // pixel center x\nconst CY: f64 = 400.0;   // pixel center y\n\nfn to_px(x: f64, y: f64) -> (i32, i32) {\n    ((CX + x * SCALE) as i32, (CY - y * SCALE) as i32)\n}\n\n// ── CR3BP equations of motion in the rotating frame ────────────────────\n\nfn derivs(s: &[f64; 4], mu: f64) -> [f64; 4] {\n    let (x, y, vx, vy) = (s[0], s[1], s[2], s[3]);\n    let x1 = -mu;\n    let x2 = 1.0 - mu;\n    let r1 = ((x - x1) * (x - x1) + y * y).sqrt();\n    let r2 = ((x - x2) * (x - x2) + y * y).sqrt();\n    let r1_3 = r1 * r1 * r1;\n    let r2_3 = r2 * r2 * r2;\n    let ax = x + 2.0 * vy - (1.0 - mu) * (x - x1) / r1_3 - mu * (x - x2) / r2_3;\n    let ay = y - 2.0 * vx - (1.0 - mu) * y / r1_3 - mu * y / r2_3;\n    [vx, vy, ax, ay]\n}\n\nfn rk4(s: &mut [f64; 4], dt: f64, mu: f64) {\n    let k1 = derivs(s, mu);\n    let mut s2 = *s;\n    for i in 0..4 { s2[i] = s[i] + 0.5 * dt * k1[i]; }\n    let k2 = derivs(&s2, mu);\n    let mut s3 = *s;\n    for i in 0..4 { s3[i] = s[i] + 0.5 * dt * k2[i]; }\n    let k3 = derivs(&s3, mu);\n    let mut s4 = *s;\n    for i in 0..4 { s4[i] = s[i] + dt * k3[i]; }\n    let k4 = derivs(&s4, mu);\n    for i in 0..4 {\n        s[i] += dt / 6.0 * (k1[i] + 2.0 * k2[i] + 2.0 * k3[i] + k4[i]);\n    }\n}\n\n// ── Newton solver for collinear L-points ───────────────────────────────\n\nfn equil_force(x: f64, mu: f64) -> f64 {\n    let x1 = -mu;\n    let x2 = 1.0 - mu;\n    let r1 = (x - x1).abs();\n    let r2 = (x - x2).abs();\n    x - (1.0 - mu) * (x - x1) / (r1 * r1 * r1) - mu * (x - x2) / (r2 * r2 * r2)\n}\n\nfn equil_deriv(x: f64, mu: f64) -> f64 {\n    let x1 = -mu;\n    let x2 = 1.0 - mu;\n    let r1 = (x - x1).abs();\n    let r2 = (x - x2).abs();\n    1.0 + 2.0 * (1.0 - mu) / (r1 * r1 * r1) + 2.0 * mu / (r2 * r2 * r2)\n        - 3.0 * (1.0 - mu) * (x - x1) * (x - x1) / (r1 * r1 * r1 * r1 * r1)\n        - 3.0 * mu * (x - x2) * (x - x2) / (r2 * r2 * r2 * r2 * r2)\n}\n\nfn solve_lx(mut x: f64, mu: f64) -> f64 {\n    for _ in 0..200 {\n        let f = equil_force(x, mu);\n        let df = equil_deriv(x, mu);\n        if df.abs() < 1e-15 { break; }\n        x -= f / df;\n    }\n    x\n}\n\n// ── Drawing helpers ────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nfn draw_circle(canvas: &mut Canvas, cx: i32, cy: i32, r: i32, color: (u8, u8, u8)) {\n    for dy in -r..=r {\n        for dx in -r..=r {\n            if dx * dx + dy * dy <= r * r {\n                let px = cx + dx;\n                let py = cy + dy;\n                if px >= 0 && py >= 0 && (px as u32) < WIDTH && (py as u32) < HEIGHT {\n                    canvas.set_pixel(px as u32, py as u32, color);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn draw_cross(canvas: &mut Canvas, cx: i32, cy: i32, size: i32, color: (u8, u8, u8)) {\n    for d in -size..=size {\n        let px = cx + d;\n        let py = cy + d;\n        let py2 = cy - d;\n        if px >= 0 && (px as u32) < WIDTH {\n            if cy >= 0 && (cy as u32) < HEIGHT {\n                canvas.set_pixel(px as u32, cy as u32, color);\n            }\n            if py >= 0 && (py as u32) < HEIGHT {\n                canvas.set_pixel(cx as u32, py as u32, color);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn draw_label(canvas: &mut Canvas, text: &str, x: i32, y: i32, color: (u8, u8, u8)) {\n    // Tiny 3x5 font for labels\n    let glyphs: &[(&str, [u8; 5])] = &[\n        (\"L\", [0b100, 0b100, 0b100, 0b100, 0b111]),\n        (\"1\", [0b010, 0b110, 0b010, 0b010, 0b111]),\n        (\"2\", [0b111, 0b001, 0b111, 0b100, 0b111]),\n        (\"3\", [0b111, 0b001, 0b111, 0b001, 0b111]),\n        (\"4\", [0b101, 0b101, 0b111, 0b001, 0b001]),\n        (\"5\", [0b111, 0b100, 0b111, 0b001, 0b111]),\n        (\"M\", [0b101, 0b111, 0b111, 0b101, 0b101]),\n    ];\n    let mut ox = 0_i32;\n    for ch in text.chars() {\n        let ch_str = ch.to_string();\n        if let Some((_, rows)) = glyphs.iter().find(|(c, _)| *c == ch_str) {\n            for (row, bits) in rows.iter().enumerate() {\n                for col in 0..3 {\n                    if bits & (1 << (2 - col)) != 0 {\n                        let px = x + ox + col;\n                        let py = y + row as i32;\n                        if px >= 0 && py >= 0 && (px as u32) < WIDTH && (py as u32) < HEIGHT {\n                            canvas.set_pixel(px as u32, py as u32, color);\n                        }\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        ox += 4;\n    }\n}\n\n// ── Simulation state ───────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nstruct Particle {\n    state: [f64; 4],\n    trail: Vec<(f64, f64)>,\n    color: (u8, u8, u8),\n    label: &'static str,\n}\n\nstruct LagrangeSim {\n    particles: Vec<Particle>,\n    lpoints: [(f64, f64); 5],\n    tick_count: u64,\n}\n\nimpl Simulation for LagrangeSim {\n    fn init() -> Self {\n        let mu = MU;\n        let x1 = -mu;\n        let x2 = 1.0 - mu;\n\n        // Compute L-points\n        let l1 = (solve_lx(0.5 * (x1 + x2), mu), 0.0);\n        let l2 = (solve_lx(x2 + 0.2, mu), 0.0);\n        let l3 = (solve_lx(x1 - 1.0, mu), 0.0);\n        let l4 = (0.5 - mu, (3.0_f64).sqrt() / 2.0);\n        let l5 = (0.5 - mu, -(3.0_f64).sqrt() / 2.0);\n        let lpoints = [l1, l2, l3, l4, l5];\n\n        // Initial conditions: small perturbation from each L-point\n        let configs: [(f64, f64, f64, f64, (u8, u8, u8), &'static str); 5] = [\n            // (dx, dy, dvx, dvy, color, label)\n            (0.004, 0.0, 0.0,  0.006, (255, 80, 80),   \"L1\"),   // L1 - red\n            (0.004, 0.0, 0.0, -0.006, (255, 160, 50),  \"L2\"),  // L2 - orange\n            (0.004, 0.0, 0.0,  0.004, (200, 80, 255),  \"L3\"),  // L3 - purple\n            (0.003, 0.003, 0.0, 0.0,  (80, 255, 120),  \"L4\"),  // L4 - green\n            (0.003, -0.003, 0.0, 0.0, (50, 200, 255),  \"L5\"),  // L5 - cyan\n        ];\n\n        let particles: Vec<Particle> = configs.iter().enumerate().map(|(i, &(dx, dy, dvx, dvy, color, label))| {\n            Particle {\n                state: [lpoints[i].0 + dx, lpoints[i].1 + dy, dvx, dvy],\n                trail: Vec::with_capacity(MAX_TRAIL),\n                color,\n                label,\n            }\n        }).collect();\n\n        Self { particles, lpoints, tick_count: 0 }\n    }\n\n    fn tick(&mut self) -> Canvas {\n        let mu = MU;\n        let speed = sim::read::<f64>(\"speed\").unwrap_or(5.0) as usize;\n\n        // Sub-step integration\n        let sub_steps = speed * 20;\n        for p in &mut self.particles {\n            for _ in 0..sub_steps {\n                rk4(&mut p.state, DT, mu);\n            }\n            // Record trail\n            p.trail.push((p.state[0], p.state[1]));\n            if p.trail.len() > MAX_TRAIL {\n                p.trail.remove(0);\n            }\n        }\n        self.tick_count += 1;\n\n        // ── Render ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n        let mut canvas = Canvas::new(WIDTH, HEIGHT);\n        canvas.fill((8, 8, 18));\n\n        // Draw faint grid\n        for i in 0..WIDTH {\n            for j in 0..HEIGHT {\n                let wx = (i as f64 - CX) / SCALE;\n                let wy = (CY - j as f64) / SCALE;\n                // Grid lines every 0.5 units\n                let gx = (wx * 2.0).fract().abs();\n                let gy = (wy * 2.0).fract().abs();\n                if gx < 0.008 || gy < 0.008 {\n                    canvas.set_pixel(i, j, (20, 20, 35));\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        // Draw axes through origin\n        let (ax0, _) = to_px(-2.2, 0.0);\n        let (ax1, _) = to_px(2.2, 0.0);\n        let (_, ay0) = to_px(0.0, -2.2);\n        let (_, ay1) = to_px(0.0, 2.2);\n        for px in ax0.max(0)..ax1.min(WIDTH as i32) {\n            canvas.set_pixel(px as u32, CY as u32, (30, 30, 50));\n        }\n        for py in ay1.max(0)..ay0.min(HEIGHT as i32) {\n            canvas.set_pixel(CX as u32, py as u32, (30, 30, 50));\n        }\n\n        // Draw unit circle (orbit of M2 in inertial frame, for reference)\n        let circle_pts = 360;\n        for k in 0..circle_pts {\n            let angle = 2.0 * std::f64::consts::PI * k as f64 / circle_pts as f64;\n            let (px, py) = to_px(angle.cos(), angle.sin());\n            if px >= 0 && py >= 0 && (px as u32) < WIDTH && (py as u32) < HEIGHT {\n                canvas.set_pixel(px as u32, py as u32, (25, 25, 45));\n            }\n        }\n\n        // Draw trails\n        for p in &self.particles {\n            let n = p.trail.len();\n            for (i, &(tx, ty)) in p.trail.iter().enumerate() {\n                let t = i as f64 / n as f64;\n                let (r, g, b) = p.color;\n                let fade = (t * t * 255.0) as u8; // quadratic fade-in\n                let cr = ((r as f64) * fade as f64 / 255.0) as u8;\n                let cg = ((g as f64) * fade as f64 / 255.0) as u8;\n                let cb = ((b as f64) * fade as f64 / 255.0) as u8;\n                let (px, py) = to_px(tx, ty);\n                if px >= 0 && py >= 0 && (px as u32) < WIDTH && (py as u32) < HEIGHT {\n                    canvas.set_pixel(px as u32, py as u32, (cr, cg, cb));\n                    // Thicken trail slightly\n                    if px + 1 < WIDTH as i32 {\n                        canvas.set_pixel(px as u32 + 1, py as u32, (cr, cg, cb));\n                    }\n                    if py + 1 < HEIGHT as i32 {\n                        canvas.set_pixel(px as u32, py as u32 + 1, (cr, cg, cb));\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        // Draw Lagrange point markers (small crosses)\n        let lp_colors: [(u8, u8, u8); 5] = [\n            (255, 80, 80), (255, 160, 50), (200, 80, 255),\n            (80, 255, 120), (50, 200, 255),\n        ];\n        let lp_labels = [\"L1\", \"L2\", \"L3\", \"L4\", \"L5\"];\n        for (i, &(lx, ly)) in self.lpoints.iter().enumerate() {\n            let (px, py) = to_px(lx, ly);\n            draw_cross(&mut canvas, px, py, 4, lp_colors[i]);\n            draw_label(&mut canvas, lp_labels[i], px + 7, py - 6, lp_colors[i]);\n        }\n\n        // Draw test particle current positions\n        for p in &self.particles {\n            let (px, py) = to_px(p.state[0], p.state[1]);\n            draw_circle(&mut canvas, px, py, 3, p.color);\n        }\n\n        // Draw primaries\n        let (m1x, m1y) = to_px(-mu, 0.0);\n        let (m2x, m2y) = to_px(1.0 - mu, 0.0);\n        draw_circle(&mut canvas, m1x, m1y, 8, (255, 220, 50));\n        draw_circle(&mut canvas, m2x, m2y, 5, (100, 180, 255));\n        draw_label(&mut canvas, \"M1\", m1x + 10, m1y - 3, (255, 220, 50));\n        draw_label(&mut canvas, \"M2\", m2x + 8, m2y - 3, (100, 180, 255));\n\n        // Publish time for dashboard\n        let t = self.tick_count as f64 / 60.0;\n        sim::emit(\"time\", &t);\n\n        canvas\n    }\n\n    fn fps() -> u32 {\n        30\n    }\n}",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_dashboard",
      "order": 3,
      "label": "Status Dashboard",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "struct Dashboard {\n    tick: u32,\n}\n\nimpl LiveView for Dashboard {\n    fn init() -> Self {\n        Self { tick: 0 }\n    }\n\n    fn tick(&mut self) -> LiveContent {\n        self.tick += 1;\n        let t = sim::read::<f64>(\"time\").unwrap_or(0.0);\n\n        LiveContent::Html(format!(\n            r#\"<div style=\"font-family:monospace;background:#0d1117;color:#e6edf3;padding:16px 20px;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid #30363d;max-width:500px\">\n  <div style=\"font-size:14px;font-weight:700;color:#58a6ff;margin-bottom:12px\">Lagrange Points — CR3BP</div>\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom:8px;font-size:13px\">\n    <div style=\"display:flex;justify-content:space-between;margin-bottom:4px\">\n      <span style=\"color:#8b949e\">Mass ratio &mu;</span>\n      <span>0.012 (Earth-Moon)</span>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;justify-content:space-between;margin-bottom:4px\">\n      <span style=\"color:#8b949e\">Frame</span>\n      <span>Co-rotating</span>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;justify-content:space-between;margin-bottom:4px\">\n      <span style=\"color:#8b949e\">Integrator</span>\n      <span>RK4</span>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;justify-content:space-between;margin-bottom:4px\">\n      <span style=\"color:#8b949e\">Elapsed time</span>\n      <span>{t:.1}s</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"border-top:1px solid #30363d;padding-top:10px;font-size:12px\">\n    <div style=\"margin-bottom:6px;font-weight:600;color:#c9d1d9\">Test Particles</div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;margin-bottom:3px\"><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;background:#ff5050;margin-right:8px\"></span><span style=\"color:#ff5050\">L1</span><span style=\"color:#8b949e;margin-left:auto\">unstable &mdash; between bodies</span></div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;margin-bottom:3px\"><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;background:#ffa032;margin-right:8px\"></span><span style=\"color:#ffa032\">L2</span><span style=\"color:#8b949e;margin-left:auto\">unstable &mdash; beyond M2</span></div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;margin-bottom:3px\"><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;background:#c850ff;margin-right:8px\"></span><span style=\"color:#c850ff\">L3</span><span style=\"color:#8b949e;margin-left:auto\">unstable &mdash; beyond M1</span></div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;margin-bottom:3px\"><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;background:#50ff78;margin-right:8px\"></span><span style=\"color:#50ff78\">L4</span><span style=\"color:#8b949e;margin-left:auto\">stable &mdash; leading triangle</span></div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;margin-bottom:3px\"><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;background:#32c8ff;margin-right:8px\"></span><span style=\"color:#32c8ff\">L5</span><span style=\"color:#8b949e;margin-left:auto\">stable &mdash; trailing triangle</span></div>\n  </div>\n</div>\"#\n        ))\n    }\n\n    fn fps() -> u32 {\n        5\n    }\n}",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_5",
      "order": 4,
      "label": "Real-World Applications",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## Real-World Applications\n\n### L1: Solar Observation\nNASA's **SOHO** and **DSCOVR** spacecraft sit near the Sun-Earth L1 point, providing uninterrupted views of the Sun and early warning of solar storms.\n\n### L2: Deep Space Telescopes\nThe **James Webb Space Telescope** orbits Sun-Earth L2, where the Sun, Earth, and Moon are all behind its sunshield — ideal for infrared astronomy. **Euclid** and **Gaia** also operate here.\n\n### L3: Science Fiction Favorite\nThe Sun-Earth L3 point (on the far side of the Sun) is a staple of sci-fi as the hiding place for a \"Counter-Earth.\" In reality, L3 is unstable and gravitational perturbations from other planets make long-term occupation impractical.\n\n### L4 & L5: Trojan Asteroids\nJupiter's L4 and L5 points harbor over **12,000 known Trojan asteroids**. NASA's **Lucy** mission is currently visiting several of these ancient objects. The Sun-Earth L4/L5 regions have also been proposed as sites for future space colonies (the **O'Neill cylinder** concept).\n\n### Station-Keeping\nThe collinear points (L1, L2, L3) are all **unstable** — spacecraft must perform periodic thruster burns to remain nearby. They typically follow **Lissajous** or **halo orbits** around the Lagrange point rather than sitting exactly on it.\n\nThe triangular points (L4, L5) are **linearly stable** when the mass ratio satisfies $\\mu < \\mu_{\\text{crit}} \\approx 0.0385$ (the Gascheau/Routh criterion). This covers all planet-Sun pairs in our solar system.",
      "cargo_toml": null
    }
  ],
  "description": "Live simulation of test particles near all five Lagrange points in the circular restricted three-body problem.",
  "tags": [
